        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="leg" value="How 10k pages become rows" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;verticalAlign=top;align=left;spacingLeft=14;spacingTop=8;fontStyle=1;fontSize=13;fontFamily=Helvetica;fillColor=#F7F9FC;strokeColor=#6C8EBF;fontColor=#6C8EBF;strokeWidth=2;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="2440" y="100" width="520" height="1420" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="legt" value="1. File lands in S3. Airflow on EC2 is notified (sensor). The EC2 box does not parse PDF bytes.&#xa;&#xa;2. Classify on ECS (image from ECR): MIME, page count, scanned vs digital.&#xa;&#xa;3. Layout LLM (Bedrock) + Textract see ONLY a sample. For a 10,000-page PDF that is ~25 pages, not 10,000. Excel: header sample. Word: first tables.&#xa;&#xa;4. LLM writes a contract JSON (columns, regions, header_row). It does not write a Python file that we exec.&#xa;&#xa;5. Extract at scale: ECS mapped tasks (PDF batches) or Glue PySpark (Excel) using that contract. That is the 'script'.&#xa;&#xa;6. Silver then gold Iceberg. Publish is one snapshot. Athena / Tableau / new PDF read gold.&#xa;&#xa;Airflow = EC2. Workers = ECS + Glue. Images = ECR. AI = sample only." style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontSize=12;fontFamily=Helvetica;fontColor=#232F3E;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="2460" y="150" width="480" height="1340" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
